 \part{Eager Tree Calculus: exact quartic proof-DAG certificates and a literal universal tree}\label{cdc:et:part}
 
-\paragraph{Source and scope.} \wtag{} This Part is manuscript~21, ``Eager Tree Calculus: Exact quartic proof-DAG certificates, operational universality, and binary sharing compression'' (batch~79, manuscript~17; archive \path{Eager_Tree_Calculus_Research_Package.zip}, inner directory \path{eager-tree-certificates}; arrival commit \texttt{aebfa386e}; no ProveIt pin, Jay's upstream commit \texttt{baa877d91} pins the semantics; placed by \texttt{a7ae02511}; shipped files prefixed \path{21-eager-tree-}; labels \texttt{cdc:et:}). It prints the manuscript's Sections 2--12 and its two appendices in their own order: a natural coding bijection of Jay's tree values with quadratic constructor equations (\cref{cdc:et:lem:bijection,cdc:et:prop:codegrowth}); the five eager value-application rules and their deterministic costs (\cref{cdc:et:def:kernel,cdc:et:lem:determinism}); proof DAGs, additive call costs as a well-foundedness certificate, the canonical reachable quotient and a cyclic counterfeit (\cref{cdc:et:lem:height,cdc:et:prop:canonical}); the fully charged quartic with $3N^2+19N$ natural witnesses and $23N+3$ quadratic residuals for an externally fixed bound $N$ on the number of distinct calls (\cref{cdc:et:thm:certificate}) and its literal gate ledger (\cref{cdc:et:prop:ledger}); a strict bracket-abstraction compiler from weak left-to-right call-by-value lambda calculus that preserves and reflects termination (\cref{cdc:et:lem:abstraction,cdc:et:thm:adequacy}); a counter-program frontend, a quoted-syntax interpreter and a literal universal tree $U$ of 175 constructor-DAG nodes with an r.e.-complete halting domain (\cref{cdc:et:prop:counter,cdc:et:lem:interpreter,cdc:et:thm:universal}), whose scalar code is charged by 175 quadratic equations (\cref{cdc:et:cor:universalpoly}); canonical certificates with empty-or-singleton natural fibres at the exact DAG size (\cref{cdc:et:thm:canonical}); row compression versus binary compression, with a fixed 108-node program $R$ whose unary inputs have $\Theta(2^n)$ unfolded calls, $O(n^2)$ witness bits and exact canonical DAG size $64n+113$ (\cref{cdc:et:thm:binarysharing,cdc:et:thm:exactgrowth}), and a general counting principle for instantiated unary templates (\cref{cdc:et:prop:unarytemplates}); the reproducibility record, six research questions and the closing remarks; and, as appendices, the two literal constructor tables and the artifact index. Its title page, abstract and Section~1 are \cref{cdc:et:sec:ms}; the conventions of this Part are \cref{cdc:et:conv}.
